# Canonicalization

Canonicalization defines a NixOS preservation layout. `$HOME` is the outer
persistence Git repository; only paths unignored by its whitelist `.gitignore`
and tracked by Git are persistent. Hosted repositories are flake submodules
located at `HOST/OWNER/REPOSITORY`.

Use `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` to distinguish the outer persistence
repository at `$HOME` from flake submodules under
`$HOME/HOST/OWNER/REPOSITORY`.

Run `git_canonicalization help` to choose an action and
`git_canonicalization help <subcommand>` before invoking a subcommand.

## Rules

- Use native Git for commits, branches, merges, remotes, fetches, and pushes.
- Use `git_canonicalization` for repository initialization, package
  scaffolding or removal, layout convergence, and exporting a validated flake
  description with `git_canonicalization status`.
- Before mutating with `git_canonicalization canonicalize`, run
  `git_canonicalization canonicalize --dry-run`. Proceed only when the reported
  changes are intended. Canonicalization may rewrite and stage managed files
  and delete ignored or untracked files.
- Do not add a `$HOME` path to the persistence whitelist merely because it
  exists at runtime. Confirm that it is intentionally persistent and track it
  before canonicalization.
- Preserve the canonical layout. Put unrestricted persistent implementation
  files in an existing permitted `prm/` tree and generated or disposable
  artifacts in an existing permitted `tmp/` tree. Consult the relevant
  subcommand help when the correct location is uncertain.
- After changing a flake submodule, run `nix fmt` and `nix flake check` from
  its root. Do not run them from `$HOME`, which is not a flake.
